package cajeta.lang;

/**
 * Policy for what to do when an encoding conversion can't represent
 * a codepoint in the target encoding (or vice versa — bytes don't
 * decode into the source encoding cleanly).
 *
 *  - FAIL   : throw EncodingException carrying enough detail to
 *             pinpoint the bad codepoint / byte position. Default.
 *  - REPAIR : substitute with the encoding's standard replacement
 *             marker (U+FFFD for UTF targets, '?' for ASCII /
 *             Latin-1 / Windows-1252, codec-specified for CJK).
 *
 * No IGNORE policy. Silent data loss is strictly worse than
 * substitution because the user has zero hint anything happened —
 * substitution at least produces detectable output. If the rare
 * "I genuinely want characters dropped" case shows up, decode-
 * iterate + filter codepoints manually before re-encoding.
 *
 * Cajeta stdlib parsing-policy enums follow this { FAIL, REPAIR }
 * shape across all parsing APIs (encoding, JSON, number parsing,
 * etc.). The semantics of REPAIR differ per domain — substitute
 * for encoding, lenient-syntax for JSON, saturate for numeric — but
 * the *shape* is uniform. See cajeta-docs/stdlib/lang/String.md §
 * Parsing convention.
 */
public enum EncodingErrorPolicy {
    FAIL,
    REPAIR
}
